<TABLE CELLSPACING=20 CELLPADDING=0 WIDTH=540>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP COLSPAN=2>
<FONT SIZE=-1>
<FONT COLOR="#D60021">7.</FONT>
<FONT COLOR="#00105A"><A HREF="../prc.htm">Product and Process Comparisons</a></FONT>
<BR>
<FONT COLOR="#D60021">7.2.</FONT>
<FONT COLOR="#00105A"><A HREF="prc2.htm">Comparisons based on data from one process</a></FONT>
<BR>
<FONT COLOR="#D60021">7.2.6.</FONT>
<FONT COLOR="#00105A"><A HREF="prc26.htm">What intervals contain a fixed percentage of the population values?</a></FONT>
<BR>
</FONT>
<BR>
<TABLE>
<TR>
<TD VALIGN=top>
<H2><FONT COLOR="#D60021">7.2.6.4.</FONT></H2>
</TD>
<TD VALIGN=top>
<H2>Two-sided tolerance intervals</H2>
</TD>
</TR >
</TABLE>
</TD>
</TR>











<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN="TOP">
<i>Two-sided tolerance intervals<i>
</TD>

<td VALIGN=TOP WIDTH="85%">
<A NAME="k2">One method for computing factors for two-sided
tolerance intervals makes use of the definition
<P>
<center>
<img src="gifs/tol2.gif" alt="k(2) = r*SQRT((N-1)/Chi-Square(gamma,N-1))">
</center>
<P>
where <i>r</i> is defined by:
<p>
<center>
<img SRC="gifs/tol3.gif" ALT=
"p = (1/SQRT(2*PI))*INTEGRAL[(1/SQRT(N))-r to (1/SQRT(N))+r]
[EXP(-t**2/2)]dt">
</center>
<P>
and
<img align=absmiddle src="gifs/chigamma.gif" alt="Chi-Square(gamma,N-1)">
is the <A HREF="../../eda/section3/eda3674.htm">critical value of the
chi-square distribution</A> with <i>N - 1</I> degrees of freedom that is
exceeded with probability, 
<img align=abscenter src="gifs/gamma.gif" alt="gamma">.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN="TOP">
<i>Interative method<i>
</TD>

<td VALIGN=TOP WIDTH="85%">
The value of <i>r</i> is estimated from the above integral
which defines limits within which <i>p</i> percent of the
<A HREF="../../eda/section7/eda3671.htm">normal distribution</A> lies.
<p>
For the same problem shown on the <A HREF="prc253.htm#example">previous
page</A>, except where <i>N</i> = 220 measurements are made of thickness,
we estimate a tolerance interval that contain a proportion
<i>p</i> = 0.90 of the wafers with probability <i>&gamma;</i> = 0.99.
<p>
The calculations used in this example can be performed using
both <a href="prc264.dp">Dataplot code</a> and <a href="prc264.r">R code</a>.
The final estimates are approximate and depend on the tolerance of the 
root-finding function or the starting value for <i>r</i>, depending on the
software being used.  The values of <i>r</i> and <i>k<sub>2</sub></i> are:
<pre>
     <i>r</i> = 1.649
     <i>k<sub>2</sub></i> = 1.853
</pre>
  

</TD>
</TR>



   

   

   

   

   
</TABLE>

